From {{inh|enm|ang|wæs}} (first/third person singular indicative past of {{m|ang|wesan}}), from {{inh|enm|gmw-pro|*was}} (first/third person singular indicative past of {{m|gmw-pro|*wesan}}). from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*was}} (first/third person singular indicative past of {{m|gem-pro|*wesaną}}).

In Kent and the West Midlands, forms with {{IPAchar|/ɛ/}} reflect Kentish and {{m+|ang-mer|wes}}, reflecting the development of {{IPAchar|/æ/}} to {{IPAchar|/e/}} found in those dialects,<ref>{{R:enm:Luick:1921-1940|I|364|352}}</ref> while elsewhere they may reflect either vowel reduction,<ref>{{R:enm:Jordan Crook:1974|32|57}}</ref> influence from {{m|enm|es|t=is}} or {{m|enm|were|id=were}}, or raising before {{IPAchar|/s/}}. The forms with {{IPAchar|/ɔ/}} characteristic of the Northwest Midland may reflect a development of {{IPAchar|/wɛ/}} to {{IPAchar|/wɔ/}} in certain environments like in {{m|enm|twolfþe|t=twelfth}} or a development of {{IPAchar|/wa/}} to {{IPAchar|/wɔ/}} in unstressed environments like in {{m|enm|quod|t=said|pos={{m+|ang|cwæþ}}}};<ref>{{R:enm:Jordan Crook:1974|265|233}}</ref> in the later language, this rounding is in the incipient stages of generalisation (but the Early Modern standard language still usually has {{IPAchar|/wæs/|/wæz/}}).<ref>{{R:en:Dobson:1957|II|4|461}}</ref>

